    "q-74184": {
      "dom_id": "step-q74184",
      "name": "Question 11",
      "problem": "Let $u=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],v=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]$ and $w=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ be vectors such that $u\\,⊥\\,v.$ Find $(u-3v+5w)\\cdot u.$",
      "worked_solution": "Since $u$ and $v$ are orthogonal vectors, we have that $u\\cdot v=0.$ Therefore, using linearity of the dot product to expand the parentheses, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(u-3v+5w)\\cdot u & =u\\cdot u-3(v\\cdot u)+5(w\\cdot u) \\\\  & =u\\cdot u-0+5(w\\cdot u) \\\\  & =u\\cdot u+5(w\\cdot u) \\\\  & =((-2)\\cdot (-2)+1\\cdot 1+0\\cdot 0+1\\cdot 1) \\\\  & +5(2\\cdot (-2)+0\\cdot 1+(-2)\\cdot 0+(-1)\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =(4+1+0+1)+5(-4+0+0-1) \\\\  & =6-25 \\\\  & =-19.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
