    "q-39333": {
      "dom_id": "step-q39333",
      "name": "Question 12",
      "problem": "Let $u=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],v=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ and $w=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$ be vectors such that $u\\,⊥\\,v.$ Find $(v+w)\\cdot u+v\\cdot (u+w).$",
      "worked_solution": "Since $u$ and $v$ are orthogonal vectors, we have that $u\\cdot v=0.$ Therefore, using linearity of the dot product to expand the parentheses, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(v+w)\\cdot u+v\\cdot (u+w) & =v\\cdot u+w\\cdot u+v\\cdot u+v\\cdot w \\\\  & =0+w\\cdot u+0+v\\cdot w \\\\  & =w\\cdot u+v\\cdot w \\\\  & =((-3)\\cdot 3+2\\cdot (-2)+5\\cdot 1+(-2)\\cdot (-2)) \\\\  & +(2\\cdot (-3)+4\\cdot 2+0\\cdot 5+(-1)\\cdot (-2)) \\\\  & =(-9-4+5+4)+(-6+8+0+2) \\\\  & =-4+4 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
