    "q-74156": {
      "dom_id": "step-q74156",
      "name": "Question 13",
      "problem": "Let $u=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],v=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $w=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$ be vectors such that $u\\,⊥\\,w.$ Find $(3w-2v)\\cdot (u+w).$",
      "worked_solution": "Since $u$ and $w$ are orthogonal vectors, we have that $u\\cdot w=0.$ Therefore, using linearity of the dot product to expand the parentheses, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3w-2v)\\cdot (u+w) & =3(w\\cdot u)-2(v\\cdot u)+3(w\\cdot w)-2(v\\cdot w) \\\\  & =0-2(v\\cdot u)+3(w\\cdot w)-2(v\\cdot w) \\\\  & =-2(v\\cdot u)+3(w\\cdot w)-2(v\\cdot w) \\\\  & =-2(3\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot (-4)+2\\cdot (-2)) \\\\  & +3((-1)\\cdot (-1)+2\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 1+(-2)\\cdot (-2)) \\\\  & -2(3\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 2+(-1)\\cdot 1+2\\cdot (-2)) \\\\  & =-2(7)+3(10)-2(-6) \\\\  & =-14+30+12 \\\\  & =28.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
