    "q-74180": {
      "dom_id": "step-q74180",
      "name": "Question 15",
      "problem": "Let $u=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],v=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ and $w=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ be vectors such that $u$ and $w$ are orthogonal. Find $(u-2v)\\cdot w+u\\cdot (v+2w).$",
      "worked_solution": "Since $u$ and $w$ are orthogonal vectors, we have that $u\\cdot w=0.$ Therefore, using linearity of the dot product to expand the parentheses, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(u-2v)\\cdot w+u\\cdot (v+2w) & =u\\cdot w-(2v)\\cdot w+u\\cdot v+u\\cdot (2w) \\\\  & =u\\cdot w-2(v\\cdot w)+u\\cdot v+2(u\\cdot w) \\\\  & =0-2(v\\cdot w)+u\\cdot v+0 \\\\  & =-2(v\\cdot w)+u\\cdot v \\\\  & =-2(2\\cdot (-2)+0\\cdot 1+(-2)\\cdot 0+(-1)\\cdot 1) \\\\  & +(2\\cdot 2+(-1)\\cdot 0+3\\cdot (-2)+5\\cdot (-1)) \\\\  & =-2(-4+0+0-1)+(4+0-6-5) \\\\  & =10-7 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
