    "q-74187": {
      "dom_id": "step-q74187",
      "name": "Question 19",
      "problem": "Let $u=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ b\\end{aligned}],v=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],$ and $w=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]$ be vectors such that $u$ is orthogonal to $v$ and $w.$ What is the value of $a+b?$",
      "worked_solution": "Given that $u$ is orthogonal to $v$ and $w$, we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v=0 \\\\ u\\cdot w=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's consider the first equation $u\\cdot v=0.$ Calculating the dot product in terms of $a$ and $b$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v & =0 \\\\ a\\cdot 5+(-1)\\cdot 0+4\\cdot -2+b\\cdot 1 & =0 \\\\ 5a+0-8+b & =0 \\\\ 5a+b & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, for the second equation $u\\cdot w=0$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot w & =0 \\\\ a\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot (-2)+4\\cdot 3+b\\cdot (-5) & =0 \\\\ a+2+12-5b & =0 \\\\ a-5b & =-14.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, to find the values of $a$ and $b$, we need to solve the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}5a+b=8 \\\\ a-5b=-14.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the first equation by $5$ and adding the result to the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n26a=26\\,⟹\\,a=1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $a=1$ into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(1)+b & =8 \\\\ 5+b & =8 \\\\ b & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have that\n\n$$\na+b=1+3=4.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
